"""Original bronchiectasis questions using the 2025 ERS framework."""
from qbank_authoring import question,ref,finalize
BR=ref('ERS: Adult Bronchiectasis Clinical Practice Guideline, 2025','https://publications.ersnet.org/content/erj/66/6/2501126')
BS=ref('FDA: Brensocatib Drug Trials Snapshot','https://www.fda.gov/drugs/drug-trials-snapshots/drug-trials-snapshots-brinsupri')
rows=[]
def q(*a,**k):rows.append(question(*a,**k))
q('Which CT finding is characteristic of bronchiectasis?', 'Bronchial dilatation with lack of normal tapering and visible peripheral airways',[
 ('Isolated interlobular septal thickening without airway change','This suggests other interstitial or edema patterns.'),('A pleural air collection without airway dilatation','This describes pneumothorax rather than bronchiectasis.'),('A solitary pulmonary artery filling defect','This is a vascular rather than bronchial abnormality.')],
 'Bronchiectasis is a structural airway diagnosis with a clinical context', '**Dilated, non-tapering bronchi are central CT features.**',['An increased bronchoarterial ratio can be supportive.','Interpret mild changes in relation to age, symptoms and reversible acute abnormalities.'], '**Assess cause and treatable traits after recognition.**',['Chronic sputum, infections and hemoptysis help define clinical burden.','Imaging distribution can suggest an underlying mechanism.'],[['Airway','Dilatation and lack of tapering'],['Clinical burden','Sputum and recurrent exacerbations'],['Next step','Etiological and severity assessment']],[BR])
q('Which baseline investigation is important in adults newly diagnosed with bronchiectasis?', 'Serum immunoglobulins and evaluation for ABPA alongside respiratory microbiology and clinical assessment',[
 ('No etiological testing because all bronchiectasis is idiopathic','Treatable causes can be missed.'),('A single normal full blood count excludes all immune disorders','Antibody deficiency can occur despite a normal blood count.'),('Only an ACE concentration in every case','That is not a sufficient core etiological evaluation.')],
 'Identifying a cause can change treatment', '**Antibody deficiency and allergic fungal disease are important treatable possibilities.**',['Review prior infections, autoimmune disease and aspiration.','Consider CF or ciliary disease when the phenotype supports them.'], '**Tailor further testing to the clinical pattern.**',['Young age, infertility or childhood ENT disease may provide clues.','Obtain relevant routine and mycobacterial sputum assessment.'],[['Core assessment','Immune, allergic and microbiological factors'],['Targeted tests','CF, ciliary or other causes'],['Purpose','Find disease-modifying opportunities']],[BR])
q('A bronchiectasis patient has chronic sinusitis since childhood, recurrent otitis, infertility and situs inversus. Which underlying disorder is most likely?', 'Primary ciliary dyskinesia',[
 ('Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency','It more classically produces emphysema and liver disease rather than this ciliary phenotype.'),('Isolated adult-onset aspiration','It does not explain the childhood ENT disease and laterality finding.'),('Acquired antibody deficiency proven by situs inversus','Laterality abnormalities favor a developmental ciliary disorder.')],
 'Lifelong mucociliary symptoms can reveal the cause of adult bronchiectasis', '**Motile ciliary dysfunction affects airway clearance and reproductive function.**',['Situs abnormalities are an important clue but are not present in every patient.','Neonatal respiratory distress can be part of the history.'], '**Refer for specialist diagnostic testing.**',['Nasal nitric oxide, genetic and ciliary studies are used in appropriate algorithms.','A single screening result may not settle the diagnosis.'],[['ENT','Chronic sinus and middle-ear disease'],['Lung','Recurrent infection and bronchiectasis'],['Other','Infertility and possible laterality defects']],[BR],style='clinical_scenario')
q('Under ERS 2025 guidance, what is the role of airway-clearance training in adult bronchiectasis?', 'It should be offered as a core individualized treatment',[
 ('It is useful only after all antibiotic options fail','Clearance is foundational rather than a last resort.'),('One technique must be prescribed identically to all patients','Technique should be individualized.'),('It replaces antibiotics in every acute bacterial exacerbation','Clearance and antimicrobial therapy address different parts of care.')],
 'Impaired mucus clearance sustains airway inflammation and infection', '**A respiratory physiotherapist can teach a suitable clearance method.**',['Adherence and feasibility matter.','Review effectiveness and adjust during exacerbations.'], '**Integrate clearance with exercise and other treatment.**',['Selected mucoactive therapy may assist difficult expectoration.','Assess bronchospasm or intolerance with inhaled treatments.'],[['Foundation','Airway-clearance training'],['Selection','Individual technique and schedule'],['Integration','Exercise and indicated pharmacotherapy']],[BR])
q('A bronchiectasis patient develops increased sputum volume and purulence, cough and breathlessness. What is an appropriate initial exacerbation approach?', 'Obtain sputum if possible and select antibiotics using previous microbiology and current severity',[
 ('Wait for culture before treating a clinically severe exacerbation','Sampling should not delay necessary therapy.'),('Use the same antibiotic indefinitely regardless of prior resistance','Prior isolates guide empiric selection.'),('Treat every episode with systemic corticosteroids alone','Steroids are not routine isolated bronchiectasis-exacerbation treatment.')],
 'Exacerbation treatment is informed by the patient’s microbial history', '**Collect a specimen before antibiotics when feasible.**',['Review prior Pseudomonas and resistance findings.','Severity and oral-treatment suitability influence route.'], '**Reassess the response and culture result.**',['A commonly used duration is 14 days, with shorter courses appropriate in selected rapidly responding cases under guidance.','Escalate for hypoxemia, systemic illness or oral treatment failure.'],[['Before therapy','Sputum when feasible'],['Empiric choice','Prior microbiology and severity'],['Review','Response and current susceptibilities']],[BR],style='clinical_scenario')
q('Under the ERS 2025 framework, which pattern can identify high future exacerbation risk warranting assessment for preventive treatment?', 'Two exacerbations in the prior year, or one severe event, or one event with severe daily symptoms',[
 ('Only three or more events qualify regardless of severity or symptoms','The updated framework is more individualized than that older fixed threshold.'),('A single asymptomatic positive culture guarantees all preventive drugs are indicated','Risk, organism and response to standard care matter.'),('Normal FEV1 excludes high future exacerbation risk','FEV1 is not the only risk dimension.')],
 'Count events but also assess their impact', '**Severe events and daily symptom burden can justify preventive assessment below three events per year.**',['Pseudomonas and other clinical traits modify risk.','Optimize standard care before long-term treatment.'], '**Choose an intervention matched to the phenotype.**',['Macrolides and selected inhaled antibiotics have distinct indications and risks.','Evaluate benefit formally after starting treatment.'],[['Events','Frequency matters'],['Severity / daily burden','Can lower the numerical threshold'],['Decision','Individual future risk after standard care']],[BR])
q('A patient has chronic Pseudomonas bronchiectasis and recurrent exacerbations despite optimized clearance. Which long-term option has a strong ERS 2025 recommendation in an appropriate high-risk patient?', 'Inhaled antibiotic treatment',[
 ('Routine inhaled corticosteroid escalation without an indication','ICS is not a substitute for infection-directed prevention.'),('Long-term fluconazole for Pseudomonas','It has no antibacterial Pseudomonas activity.'),('No preventive assessment because only IV drugs can affect airway infection','Inhaled strategies can provide relevant local treatment.')],
 'Chronic infection and exacerbation risk define a treatment population', '**Long-term inhaled antibiotics can reduce exacerbations in selected chronic Pseudomonas disease.**',['Assess airway tolerability and bronchospasm risk.','Macrolides are also a first-line preventive option in suitable high-risk patients under the updated framework.'], '**Treat for a defined period and evaluate response.**',['Track exacerbations, symptoms and adverse effects.','Stop or modify ineffective or poorly tolerated treatment.'],[['Phenotype','Chronic Pseudomonas plus high risk'],['Option','Long-term inhaled antibiotic'],['Review','Effectiveness and tolerability']],[BR],style='clinical_scenario')
q('Before starting long-term azithromycin for bronchiectasis, which assessment is particularly important?', 'Exclude active nontuberculous mycobacterial infection and review QT, hearing and interaction risks',[
 ('A normal chest radiograph alone excludes NTM disease','NTM assessment needs appropriate microbiological and clinical evaluation.'),('Macrolide monotherapy is preferred for known active MAC pulmonary disease','It risks selecting macrolide resistance.'),('No baseline safety review is necessary because the dose is preventive','Long-term exposure still carries important harms.')],
 'Preventive macrolides can compromise future NTM treatment if used carelessly', '**Do not give macrolide monotherapy for unrecognized active NTM disease.**',['Obtain suitable mycobacterial specimens when assessing eligibility.','Review ECG-related risk and hearing history.'], '**Use a monitored treatment trial in the correct high-risk population.**',['Track exacerbation response and adverse effects.','Reassess if new features suggest NTM or another cause of deterioration.'],[['Microbiology','Assess active NTM'],['Safety','QT, hearing and interactions'],['Follow-up','Benefit and toxicity']],[BR])
q('A first Pseudomonas isolation occurs in an adult with bronchiectasis. What is an appropriate discussion?', 'Consider a specialist-guided eradication strategy rather than automatically accepting chronic infection',[
 ('A single isolate proves lifelong irreversible infection','Early eradication may be considered.'),('No action is ever indicated if the patient is currently stable','The finding can affect future risk and deserves review.'),('An antifungal regimen is the specific eradication strategy','Pseudomonas requires antibacterial treatment.')],
 'New acquisition and established chronic infection are different situations', '**ERS conditionally supports eradication after new Pseudomonas isolation.**',['Assess the clinical state and susceptibility profile.','Discuss expected benefit, burden and uncertainty.'], '**Confirm subsequent microbiological status.**',['Failure to eradicate may lead to a chronic-infection pathway.','Continue clearance and other foundational care.'],[['New isolation','Consider eradication'],['Chronic infection','Risk-based suppressive strategies'],['Follow-up','Repeat microbiology and clinical assessment']],[BR],style='clinical_scenario')
q('Which statement about inhaled corticosteroids in bronchiectasis is most appropriate?', 'They are not routinely indicated solely for bronchiectasis without another relevant indication such as asthma',[
 ('All bronchiectasis requires high-dose ICS','Routine use does not follow from the structural diagnosis alone.'),('Coexisting asthma must never be treated with ICS','Asthma remains a relevant indication.'),('ICS directly eradicates Pseudomonas','It is not an antibacterial treatment.')],
 'Treat the inflammatory phenotype rather than assuming one inhaler fits every airway disease', '**An independent indication should guide ICS use.**',['Asthma and selected COPD phenotypes may justify treatment.','Avoid unnecessary steroid exposure and infection-related risks.'], '**Reconcile the complete airway diagnosis.**',['Wheeze alone does not establish asthma.','Review objective evidence and prior treatment response.'],[['Bronchiectasis alone','No routine ICS'],['Coexisting asthma','ICS may be essential'],['Infection','Needs separate management']],[BR])
q('What is the mechanism of brensocatib, approved in the United States for non-CF bronchiectasis in patients aged 12 years and older?', 'Dipeptidyl peptidase-1 inhibition, reducing activation of neutrophil serine proteases',[
 ('CFTR potentiation','That is a different mechanism used in selected CF treatment.'),('Direct Pseudomonas cell-wall inhibition','Brensocatib is not an antibacterial beta-lactam.'),('IgE binding','That describes an anti-IgE biologic pathway.')],
 'A newer bronchiectasis treatment targets neutrophilic injury', '**DPP1 participates in activation of neutrophil serine proteases.**',['Brensocatib targets this inflammatory pathway.','Its US indication is non-cystic fibrosis bronchiectasis.'], '**Use the current local label and eligibility assessment.**',['Approval does not replace airway clearance or indicated infection treatment.','Monitor tolerability and drug-specific precautions.'],[['Target','DPP1'],['Effect','Reduced protease activation'],['Population','US-labeled non-CF bronchiectasis, age at least 12']],[BS])
q('Which CF treatment should not be routinely extrapolated to non-CF bronchiectasis because trials showed harm or lack of benefit?', 'Recombinant human DNase as routine maintenance treatment',[
 ('Individualized airway-clearance techniques','These are core bronchiectasis care.'),('Pulmonary rehabilitation for exercise limitation','This has a recommended role.'),('Culture-directed treatment of a bacterial exacerbation','This is a standard clinical strategy.')],
 'Shared sputum symptoms do not make CF and non-CF treatment evidence interchangeable', '**Dornase alfa is useful in CF but is not recommended routinely in non-CF bronchiectasis.**',['The airway biology and trial outcomes differ.','Other mucoactive options require their own assessment.'], '**Use population-specific evidence.**',['Assess clearance technique and hydration of secretions.','Monitor response and bronchospasm with any inhaled intervention.'],[['CF','DNase has an established role'],['Non-CF bronchiectasis','Do not routinely extrapolate'],['Principle','Disease-specific trial evidence']],[BR])
